\chapter{A Fellowship Around a Small Machine}
\label{ch:origins}
\index{Bell Laboratories}\index{Multics}\index{Thompson, Ken}\index{Ritchie, Dennis}

\section{What was lost when Multics went away}

Unix began at Bell Laboratories, not at an institution called ``Unix Labs.''
In 1969, Bell Labs was withdrawing from Multics, the ambitious time-sharing
project undertaken with MIT and General Electric. Multics proposed a
computing utility: many people would share a powerful installation through
interactive terminals, with persistent files and carefully structured
protection. Its ambitions were not absurd. Many later systems would adopt
ideas it explored. The difficulty for Bell Labs was the expense and slow
arrival of a service that met immediate needs.

For Ken Thompson, Dennis Ritchie, Doug McIlroy, and Joe Ossanna, withdrawal
also meant losing an appealing way to work. An interactive system was more
than a faster replacement for submitting decks of punched cards. It let
people keep programs, documents, and unfinished thoughts in a common place.
Ritchie's account emphasizes the wish to preserve a fellowship around
computing. That social purpose helps explain why a small operating system
could become a large cultural influence. Its users were also its authors,
and improvement could begin as a conversation down a corridor
\parencite[``Origins'']{evolution}.

This does not mean that Unix emerged in a vacuum of unlimited freedom.
Computers required budgets, management approval, maintenance, and a
credible reason to exist. Proposals for a substantial replacement machine
failed to produce the desired purchase. The available opportunity was a
little-used PDP-7. Thompson's experiments with a space-travel game formed
part of the immediate setting, but the game is an inadequate explanation
for the operating system. A game could motivate graphics work without
explaining directories, process creation, or a community's programming
practice.

The useful historical question is therefore not which amusing incident
``caused Unix.'' It is how limited hardware and a particular community
encouraged a workable set of compromises. The participants wanted an
interactive environment they could understand and alter. They could not
build the full computing utility promised by Multics. They could implement
an environment that made their own daily work better.

\section{The PDP-7 was a beginning, not the finished design}

The earliest system was written in assembly language. It established parts
of the file and process model, but it was not a miniature copy of a modern
Linux distribution. Ritchie describes successive experiments in file
naming, links, and process control. Features now presented as a timeless
Unix philosophy were often the results of replacing something awkward with
something easier to use. Reading the early history as a sequence of
revisions gives the designers more credit than imagining they possessed a
complete blueprint from the outset.

The file hierarchy gave users a way to organize persistent objects. A
process gave a running computation an identity and an environment. A
command interpreter let users request work without making each command a
special operation inside the kernel. These abstractions reinforced one
another. A command could name an ordinary file, become a process, and
operate on other files. New commands could be added without redesigning the
operating system's central control path.

The division between the kernel and the command environment was especially
consequential. Hardware protection and resource arbitration belonged in the
kernel. Most choices about useful work could be expressed by ordinary
programs. An editor was indispensable to the environment, but it did not
need privileged knowledge of how the scheduler worked. This separation
made the system expandable by people who were not kernel authors.

Early Unix nevertheless carried constraints that later readers should not
romanticize. Memory and disk space were scarce, peripherals were slow, and
crashes could destroy work. A terse interface saved typing on slow
terminals, but it also made commands hard to discover. Some of the features
that feel elegant in retrospect were successful accommodations to severe
limitations. Their survival does not prove that every limitation deserved
to survive with them.

\section{The PDP-11 and a practical constituency}
\index{PDP-11}\index{patent applications}

The move to a PDP-11 made a larger future possible. The early PDP-11/20
installation was still modest and lacked the memory protection of later
machines. Its justification included document preparation for the patent
department. An editor and formatter were therefore not optional
accessories. They were part of the reason the machine could be acquired
and kept useful.

Ritchie recalls supporting three patent typists during the latter half of
1971 while the developers continued experimenting with the system. Their
requirements were concrete: mathematical symbols and line-numbered pages
for patent applications. A formatter could be adapted quickly to provide a
feature that a competing commercial system did not provide. This was a
small demonstration of a general advantage: ownership of the implementation
could make an unusual local requirement inexpensive to satisfy
\parencite[discussion of the PDP-11]{evolution}.

The experiment was precarious. A programming mistake could bring down the
same machine on which typists were doing productive work. The disk was
small enough that material had to be moved to tape frequently. Yet the
service worked well enough to win institutional support for a more capable
PDP-11/45. Unix's first expansion thus involved evidence of usefulness,
not only persuasion about architectural elegance.

The 1974 paper by Ritchie and Thompson describes a system already serving
multiple installations and tasks: patent work, telephone-service data,
research, programming, and document preparation. It reports the PDP-11
system of its own period. Its hardware figures and limits must not be
silently transferred backward to the PDP-7 or forward to Sixth Edition.
An edition label is a historical coordinate, not a decorative suffix
\parencite[secs. 1--2]{ritchie-thompson}.

\section{Why C changed what could travel}
\index{C language}\index{portability}

Thompson's B, derived from BCPL, preceded C. B's word-oriented model and
implementation did not fit every aspect of the byte-addressed PDP-11
comfortably. Ritchie's work on C added a type system and operations suited
to expressing the data structures and machine-level work the kernel
required. The important result was not simply a more convenient syntax.
It was the ability to describe most of an operating system in a form that
could be retargeted to another machine.

The 1973 rewrite of the kernel in C is a major turning point, but ``written
in C'' does not mean independent of hardware. A kernel still needs startup
code, trap entry, context switching, access to device registers, and
knowledge of memory-management hardware. C also permits assumptions about
word sizes, byte order, alignment, and representation that can make a
program effectively machine-specific. Portability had to be engineered
through interfaces and disciplined assumptions; a language merely made that
engineering more tractable.

Later ports, including work on Interdata machines, tested the claim in a
way that repeating it on PDP-11s could not. A compiler could absorb many
instruction-set differences. Kernel code could retain the organization of
processes, files, and devices while selected machine-dependent sections
changed. That arrangement would become a template for later operating
systems and for the software industries built around them
\parencite[``Portability'']{kernighan-history}.

C and Unix also formed a feedback loop. Unix offered a productive place to
implement compilers and tools. C offered a compact way to extend Unix.
Programs written for that environment spread the language, while a growing
population of C programmers made new Unix software easier to produce.
Neither language popularity nor operating-system adoption has a single
cause, but this mutual reinforcement is difficult to miss.

\section{A publication becomes an invitation}

The public presentation in 1973 and the revised paper in 1974 made Unix
legible to a wider technical audience. Its claims were unusually tangible:
a hierarchical file system, compatible I/O for different objects, separate
processes, a replaceable command language, and an extensive collection of
programs. A reader could recognize a complete working environment rather
than an isolated research mechanism.

Distribution outside Bell Labs depended on licensing. The legal and
business setting of AT\&T encouraged a form of software circulation very
different from selling a fully supported consumer product. Universities
could obtain source under license, study it, and exchange knowledge with
other authorized users. They did not thereby acquire the unrestricted
redistribution rights associated with later free software. Source
availability and freedom to redistribute are separate historical facts.

That distinction matters when celebrating Unix as an ancestor of open
source. Early participants built a vigorous sharing culture, but its legal
boundaries excluded people. The desire to teach from the kernel would later
make those boundaries painfully visible in the story of Lions's commentary.
The small system was easy enough to explain; permission to circulate the
explanation was another matter.

By the middle of the 1970s, Unix had several foundations for expansion:
a credible practical workload, a comprehensible kernel, a productive
language, and users willing to teach one another. It also had a particular
view of composition. The next two chapters follow that view into printed
pages and command lines, where some of its most durable inventions became
part of ordinary work.
